[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, obviously YouTube\'s filled with an insane amount of incredible ideas and projects. And every once in a while, I come across a video that\'s truly inspiring, but often times overlooked. Now, I\'m going to be starting a series where once a month I feature one of these projects on my channel in hopes of'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'getting that creator more recognition and visibility. And you\'re all in for a treat because here in episode 1, I\'ll be showcasing a project from who I would consider to be an absolute genius in the 3D printing and design space, Andy Hwitt. Now, make sure to watch the video'

[00:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to the end so YouTube will recommend this to others, which will help more people see his work. And check out the description for a discount on his 3D print file and a link to his channel to see more of his incredible designs. So, I was looking for an easier way to'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'route addressable strips on my hex grid and thought about an idea I had when I was developing duality hex grid. The idea was to split the segments into two lanes with a divider to create a pattern of individual nested hexagons'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'rather than a hexagon grid where each hexagon side is shared with its neighbors. I decided against this early on because I thought it would complicate the routing of addressable strips. But when I started looking into it, I'

[01:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'discovered something fascinating. By making each hexagon its own, we can run a strip through all six hexagon segments and it will return to the same spot with a data in end and a data out end for'

[01:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'each hexagon. Then all we need to do is connect the hexagons in series. Which means what we have here is a nested hexagon matrix display.'

[01:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Think of it as a matrix display with hexagonal elements where you can not only control the change of color of each element, but also its pattern of light. It\'s both a matrix display and an etch'

[01:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sketch. You can connect your hexagons however you want, but I will show you how I chose to run mine. I chose a symmetrical 10 hexagon pattern. Each hexagon will be made of'

[01:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'six 200 mm strips for a total of 24 pixels per hex and a total of 240 pixels. My perimeter will have 22 200 mm strips'

[02:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for a total of 88 pixels. I will be wiring my hexagons in this order. Since the power wires don\'t need to run in series like the data wires do, I\'m'

[02:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'only going to route the data wires where they need to go and connect my power wires where it\'s most convenient. I will be connecting these where two hexes are next to each other. I\'ll create three columns of hex\'s and then'

[02:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'connect the three columns together. The split segments have a divider in the middle to create two lanes along with clearance for the mounting screws and for wires to cross between the lanes. The skins or diffusers are divided as'

[02:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'well and include tabs to cover the clearance area to keep the light from each lane isolated while still allowing wires to pass at the bottom. The B segments are for the inner part of the grid and look similar to the duality hex grid,'

[02:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but since the segments are split, there will be an empty lane on the outer perimeter segments. This will allow us to add one continuous strip around the perimeter to frame and accent our patterns. I actually chose to use two wired strip assemblies. starting'

[03:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and ending right on the center line to make it symmetrical and ease installation. Segments A, C, and D are for the perimeter of the grid and now have mitered ends where they create an'

[03:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'outside corner. This will allow our perimeter strip to easily pass through the segments without disrupting the inner hexagons. [music] Having two LED strips at the bottom means a unique design for the base segment. I created three different base options. The first'

[03:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is the most compact and is designed for the seed [music] studio ESP32C3 as well as a buck converter and an optional logic level shifter. The second is a developer base with enough room for a full-size ESP32 and or other'

[03:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'components you may want to add such as buttons or sensors. The third base is the largest and should fit an array of many different WLED controllers. You could also skip the base and have your'

[04:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'controller mounted away from your display. Also, due to the dual strip [music] configuration, I created flange two-way and three-way connectors. If you\'re planning to use screws to mount your display, I use the term display lightly cuz this could literally be an'

[04:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'entire wall. If you don\'t want to have a perimeter strip, you don\'t [music] have to. You can simply use the single lane perimeter segment ACD for all your perimeter segments. There are also ACD base segments. [music] In doing this,'

[04:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you will save on material and time. I\'m demonstrating the perimeter strip version [music] because in the end, you\'ll know enough to do either version. After doing this one, I would say go without the perimeter strip [music] and'

[04:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'use those resources to make your display bigger. The perimeter strip is a nice touch, though, and you can choose what works best for you. The compact base is easy to assemble, especially if you follow'

[04:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this diagram. It\'s recommended to include a shotkey diode at the 5volt pin of the ESP32. I chose to leave it out on mine because I won\'t be using the USB port when the'

[05:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'strips are powered. You basically just mount everything to'

[05:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the back plane and then slide it into the base. I chose to use a logic level shifter because with the smaller ESP32s, I usually end up having to add one. The logic shifter just converts the'

[05:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ESP32 3.3 volt logic to 5V logic to make sure all the highs are in the 5V threshold. I\'m using pin D2 for my hexagons [music] and pin D6 for my perimeter.'

[05:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Think of the LED strips [music] as the power source for the base circuit. I will just solder Vin from the buck inverter to 12vt on the strip and ground [music] to ground.'

[06:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I added wires between the segments for a cleaner look and more flexibility using the hex grid wire jig. [music] You can also skip this step and run one continuous strip for each hexagon, but make [music] sure to use the narrower 5'

[06:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mm strips. I started running my hexagon strips on the corner opposite from the corner where the data in and data out ends [music] will be. I pre-bent my wires for the corners to provide strain relief and set the distance. Once I got the strips in place, I'

[06:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'removed the adhesive cover strip. Again, starting in the opposite corner and working my way towards [music] each end. When I connected the hexagons together, I routed the data out wire and data in wire from the next hexagon towards each'

[06:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'other, then twisted them together in between the two cable retainers. I really like this silicone putty for cable management. It also works well for blocking light where you don\'t want it. Soldered my'

[06:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '12vt and ground wires to opposite ends of the segments to minimize disruption of the LEDs. Before I [music] installed the strips, I tinned the pads with a large amount of solder.'

[07:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I found it very easy to solder all the power wires in place. You can always protect the plastic with something like aluminum foil, but I found just paying attention does the job as well. [music]'

[07:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'After all my hexagon\'s power rails were connected, I connected the perimeter strip to power to the hexagon circuit in four evenly spaced locations. To power my display, I just drilled a hole in the base and ran 18 gauge cable through it'

[07:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and soldered to two solid wires I had already connected to [music] the strip. The tabs on the diffusers are very thin because they have to be. I did not have any issues with breakage.'

[07:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Just keep everything lined up when installing and removing and everything [music] will be fine. I installed WLED on my ESP32 from the WLED install page via a USBC [music]'

[07:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'data cable. I installed the WLED app on my phone. In LED preferences, I selected GPIO4 for my hexagon strips. I set my length and strip type. I click the plus'

[08:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sign to add perimeter [music] strip using GPIO 21. set the length to 88 pixels and selected my strip type. Under segments, I created a segment for my hexagon matrix. Since each hexagon is 24'

[08:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'pixels, [music] I grouped them in 24s. So, each hex is one element. I added another segment for the perimeter, but [music] kept the grouping'

[08:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'at one. Everything you\'re seeing here is just standard WLED effects in the app. Once you\'ve built your display, you can'

[08:45] [scene:talk]
